W = 8, declared domain = 0..256 (exhaustive)

SAME MULTISET, TWO ORDERS
  A: *3 then +200 steps=[MulK(3), AddK(200)]
                 per-step widths = [10, 10], chain needs 10 bits
  B: +200 then *3 steps=[AddK(200), MulK(3)]
                 per-step widths = [9, 11], chain needs 11 bits
  -> A needs 10 bits, B needs 11 bits, from the same two steps.

THE SAME STEP, TWO REQUIREMENTS
  *3 as the first step needs  10 bits
  *3 as the second step needs 11 bits

CONTROL: commuting steps must report the SAME requirement
  C: *3 then *5  steps=[MulK(3), MulK(5)]
                 per-step widths = [10, 12], chain needs 12 bits
  D: *5 then *3  steps=[MulK(5), MulK(3)]
                 per-step widths = [11, 12], chain needs 12 bits
  -> both need 12 bits, as they must.

CONTROL: the reported width must be TIGHT
  A: fits(10) = true, fits(9) = false
  B: fits(11) = true, fits(10) = false
  C: fits(12) = true, fits(11) = false

HOW FAR THE GAP GOES: the same step multiset, worst and best order
  24 orderings of the same four steps: narrowest 11 bits, widest 13 bits

RESULT: the carrier requirement is a property of the sequence. Both controls behaved as required.
